{
  "reviews": {
    "node-a2rilu": {
      "supported": false,
      "issue": "The node claims 'RUN' remained active despite pressure loss to rule out mechanical seizure as a primary cause of P-R drop. The evidence (IA-P02) defines C-11 RUN as an auxiliary contact indicating motor energization but explicitly states it is not a local flow proof and does not establish the state of any distribution valve or prove that every supply disturbance would appear in the sample export.",
      "evidenceNeeded": "Evidence establishing whether mechanical seizure could occur while the motor was running (e.g., bearing failure, internal blockage) OR evidence confirming that pressure loss at P-R is solely due to upstream/downstream flow interruption rather than equipment failure. The current record only proves the motor ran; it does not prove no other cause contributed.",
      "question": "Does any source establish whether mechanical seizure can occur while a compressor runs, or was there an inspection/record confirming internal valve condition during maintenance preparation?"
    },
    "node-gjj8e6": {
      "supported": false,
      "issue": "The node infers the issue location (downstream of H-U) based on P-U stability vs. P-R drop using IA-P02 point register definitions. However, IA-D02 states that a pressure tag names only one measurement point and the point register is needed to interpret scope; it does not establish valve states or guarantee coverage for all disturbances.",
      "evidenceNeeded": "A continuous waveform or additional sampled values from intermediate points (North gallery) OR an explicit record confirming P-U stability at H-U during the incident timeframe. The current evidence only defines what P-U and P-R represent, not their actual measured behavior relative to each other.",
      "question": "Is there a pressure extract containing simultaneous samples for both P-U and P-R that confirms upstream stability while downstream dropped?"
    },
    "node-1huj6bi": {
      "supported": true,
      "issue": "",
      "evidenceNeeded": "",
      "question": ""
    }
  },"decision":{
    "summary":"Two nodes (a2rilu and gjj8e6) are unsupported because their conclusions rely on assumptions not established by the supplied evidence. Node a2rilu assumes 'RUN' active excludes mechanical seizure without proof that such failure is impossible while running or that no other cause existed. Node gjj8e6 infers location from point definitions alone, ignoring IA-D02's warning about single-point tags and lack of valve-state coverage.",
    "evidenceUsed":["da0260c0-7d49-43e8-b074-da2a14c7d59c#1","d838e866-dc34-4814-8dad-3b97d0353f05#1"],"unknowns":["Actual cause of P-R drop (source vs. distribution vs. equipment)","Whether mechanical seizure could occur while motor ran","Simultaneous pressure readings at intermediate points"],"alternatives":["Pressure loss originated upstream and propagated downstream without local failure.","A valve closed during maintenance preparation caused the drop, but no record confirms its state."],"confidence":0.85}
}
